## Final Judgement
## Conclusion

The key findings from the fact-check are as follows: Nigeria gained independence on October 1, 1960, confirming the "60 years of existence" as of October 1, 2020. The documented periods of military rule are: (1) January 1966 to July 1975 (~9.5 years), (2) July 1975 to October 1979 (~4 years), and (3) December 1983 to May 1999 (~15.5 years). These three periods sum to approximately 29 cumulative years of military rule. A corroborating source (Refworld) states that "The military has ruled Nigeria for all but ten years since independence from Britain in 1960," written around 1999–2000 (39 years after independence), which implies 29 years of military rule—exactly matching Buhari's claim. Since Nigeria returned to civilian rule in May 1999 and remained under civilian governance through October 2020, no additional years of military rule accumulated, keeping the total at 29. The claim's attribution to President Buhari is also confirmed.

Based on the documented historical record and corroborating sources, the cumulative total of 29 years of military rule out of 60 years of independence is well-supported by the evidence.

