### Justification
Nigeria gained independence on October 1, 1960, and by October 1, 2020, had existed as a nation for 60 years. Three documented periods of military rule—January 1966 to July 1975 (~9.5 years), July 1975 to October 1979 (~4 years), and December 1983 to May 1999 (~15.5 years)—sum to approximately 29 cumulative years. A [Refworld source](https://www.refworld.org/docid/5278c70012.html) from around 1999–2000 corroborates this, stating that "The military has ruled Nigeria for all but ten years since independence from Britain in 1960," which, at roughly 39 years after independence, implies 29 years of military rule. Since Nigeria returned to civilian governance in May 1999 and remained civilian through 2020, no further military years were added. The claim's attribution to President Buhari in his [October 1, 2020 Independence Day speech](https://statehouse.gov.ng/news/independence-day-address-by-president-muhammadu-buhari/) is also confirmed.